    "turn_text": "The red card bill was introduced back in 2006. I think the timing was just off. We re-introduced it. You know, we've modified a little bit, but what this is is a non-citizen work permit. This is not a path on amnesty. It's not a path to citizenship, but it allows us to maintain a labor force that we need as we continue to build and grow our economy. However, if you don't have a red card, you will be deported. We're going to ask you, why are you here? And if you're not here to work and contribute to our society, then obviously you're here for other reasons. I support mass deportation like President Trump is saying, look, when you have over 10 million people come on into our country over the last four years, that's an invasion. We don't even know who they are. They've lost children. They've lost children throughout the country. And we know that there's a high rise in sex trafficking, child trafficking, and all those types of things. So the red card would allow us to properly manage, once the border is closed, which President Trump is going to do, to properly manage the inflow of immigrants that are wanting to come here and work. And here's the deal. Joe, it's not forever. They have two distinct periods that they must apply to become citizens. If they do not apply to become citizens, then when that time expires, guess what? They get deported. And we bring in others that want to have the same opportunity. So it is a foundation by which we can build on to make sure that we have a labor force. But more importantly, we close the border and we know who's in our country.",
